class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* removeNthFromEnd(ListNode* head, int n) {
        ListNode* dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy->next = head;
        
        ListNode* fast = dummy;
        ListNode* slow = dummy;
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            fast = fast->next;
        }
        while (fast != nullptr) {
            fast = fast->next;
            slow = slow->next;
        }
        ListNode* temp = slow->next;
        slow->next = slow->next->next;
        delete temp;
        
        return dummy->next;
    }
};
Approach — Remove Nth Node From End of List

The main idea is to use the Two Pointer / Fast and Slow Pointer technique.

We use a dummy node before head to make removing the first node easier.

1. Create a Dummy Node
ListNode* dummy = new ListNode(0);
dummy->next = head;

The list becomes:

dummy → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

This helps when the node to remove is the head.

2. Initialize Two Pointers
ListNode* fast = dummy;
ListNode* slow = dummy;

Both start at the dummy node.

3. Move fast Ahead by n + 1
for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
    fast = fast->next;
}

We move fast n+1 positions ahead of slow.

Why n+1?

Because slow needs to stop at the node just before the node we want to delete.

Example:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
            ↑
          remove 4

If n = 2, the 2nd node from the end is 4.

After creating the gap:

slow                  fast
 ↓                      ↓
dummy → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
4. Move Both Pointers
while (fast != nullptr) {
    fast = fast->next;
    slow = slow->next;
}

Both move at the same speed until fast reaches the end.

Now slow is immediately before the node to remove.

5. Remove the Node
ListNode* temp = slow->next;
slow->next = slow->next->next;
delete temp;

Suppose:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
        ↑   ↑
      slow remove

After:

slow->next = slow->next->next;

we get:

1 → 2 → 3 → 5
6. Return the Head
return dummy->next;

We return the actual head, not the dummy node.

Time Complexity
O(n)

There are two traversals:

Moving fast → O(n)
Moving fast and slow → O(n)

Therefore:

O(n) + O(n) = O(n)

Since constants are ignored:

Time Complexity = O(n)

Space Complexity

Only a few pointers are used:

dummy
fast
slow
temp

So:

Space Complexity = O(1)
